## Reasoning

**Entity types present in the schema.** Every record is one ADS-B state-vector message: it names an airframe (`icao24`, `callsign`, `squawk`), a moment in time (`time`), a position (`lat`, `lon`), a motion state (`velocity`, `heading`, `vertrate`), and two altitudes (`baroaltitude`, `geoaltitude`). So the schema is not one flat entity: the ontology models several of these as their own classes exactly one object-property hop away.

**Aircraft.** The stable identity is `icao24` — its description says it "identifies one specific aircraft and does not change during a registration period", i.e. it is the airframe key and is populated in every record. Class: `bsm:Aircraft` (a `bsm:Vehicle`, which carries `bsm:hasObservation`). Subject expression `aircraft_{icao24}`.

**Observation.** `bsm:Vehicle` has `bsm:hasObservation` → `bsm:Observation` with `bsm:observationDateTime`. A timeseries row *is* an observation of the aircraft at `time`, so `time` is not a literal on the aircraft; it identifies an Observation. Subject `observation_{icao24}_{time}` (unique per airframe per timestamp; both columns always populated).

**Location + Absolute Point.** `bsm:Observation` inherits `bsm:hasGeographicLocation` → `bsm:Location`, which has `bsm:hasGeometry` → geometry; `bsm:AbsolutePoint` carries exactly `bsm:latitudeDecimal` / `bsm:longitudeDecimal` and the columns say "decimal WGS84 coordinate". So `lat`/`lon` become an AbsolutePoint under a Location, not literals on the aircraft.

**Velocity.** Unattached `bsm:hasVelocity` → `bsm:Velocity` with `bsm:speedMetersPerSecond` (matches `velocity`, "speed over ground … in metres per second" — units agree exactly).

**The heading ambiguity (decisive).** Two near-synonym candidates: `bsm:headingAngle` on `bsm:Orientation` versus `bsm:absoluteBearingAngle` on `bsm:Velocity`. The qualifier that decides is nose-attitude versus course-over-ground, and the column description resolves it explicitly: "Despite the column name this is the track angle over ground rather than the aircraft's nose heading." A track angle measured clockwise from geographic north is an absolute bearing of the motion vector, so `heading` maps to `bsm:absoluteBearingAngle` on the Velocity, and `bsm:Orientation`/`bsm:headingAngle` is deliberately not used — no column reports airframe attitude.

**Altitude.** `bsm:hasAltitude` → `bsm:Altitude` with `bsm:altitudeMeters`. There are two independent measurements (barometric and GNSS), both in metres, so they are two distinct Altitude individuals hung off the same Observation, keyed `baroaltitude_{icao24}_{time}` and `geoaltitude_{icao24}_{time}`.

**vertrate.** `bsm:Velocity` offers `bsm:inclinationAngle`, but that is an *angle* while `vertrate` is a vertical *speed* in m/s; the qualifier disagrees, so I treat it as unmatched rather than forcing it. Unmapped.

**Squawk.** `bsm:AlternateIdentifier` (standard code + value) is exactly the "one entity carrying codes under several schemes" shape, and here the schema genuinely evidences multiple distinct identification schemes for the same airframe — the ICAO 24-bit transponder address, the broadcast callsign, and the ATC-assigned 4-digit octal squawk. `squawk` is the one that has no other home, so it becomes an `bsm:AlternateIdentifier` (value = the code, standard code = a constant naming the scheme) reached by `bsm:hasAlternateIdentifier`. Moderate confidence, since the standard-code side is supplied as a constant.

**Remaining columns.** `callsign` → `bsm:alternateName` (an additional broadcast name for the airframe, not its stable identity). `icao24` also serves as `bsm:conceptName` for the aircraft. `onground` (surface vs airborne broadcast) is the closest fit to `bsm:operatingModeCode` on `bsm:Vehicle`, but this is weak evidence and the column is described as possibly absent — low confidence.

**IRI transformation check.** `hasObservation` → `observation_{icao24}_{time}`; `hasGeographicLocation` → `location_{icao24}_{time}`; `hasGeometry` → `point_{icao24}_{time}`; `hasVelocity` → `velocity_{icao24}_{time}`; `hasAltitude` → `baroaltitude_{icao24}_{time}` and `geoaltitude_{icao24}_{time}`; `hasAlternateIdentifier` → `squawk_{icao24}_{squawk}`. Each string was written once for the subject and copied verbatim into the referencing value.

